  "rationale": "The example is covered by one `{test}` reference, at `tests/outcomes.rs:204`, on `fixme_does_not_hide_stale_inputs_or_uncovered_references`.\n\n**Setup.** The `fixture()` function (`tests/outcomes.rs` ~lines 11–62) writes `observed.txt` with a `// FIXME(#125): …` comment. It also writes a `timeout` example record with determination `{\"FIXME\": [\"#125\", \"rust-lang/rust#123\"]}`. That record has a file-range input (lines 1–1) covering the comment. The `fixme_examples_pass_without_test_references_but_true_examples_do_not` test (~line 116) asserts that the unmodified fixture validates with no errors. That gives the \"current record that passes CI\" precondition.\n\n**Scenario.** The test overwrites `observed.txt` with `changed source`, so the recorded comment text no longer matches. It then asserts the following:\n- `validate_repository` reports an error containing \"input text changed\", so the changed input is reported even though the determination is still FIXME.\n- `status_markdown` shows the `requirements.timeout` row as `Invalid`.\n- The status output still mentions `#125` and the \"input text changed\" message.\n\nThe test also writes an `{impl}` reference in `other.txt` and checks that validation reports \"no input covers … other.txt:1\". This shows FIXME does not excuse uncovered references, which supports the `requirements.ci.determination` context.\n\n**Remaining gaps.**\n- The test replaces the whole file rather than editing only the comment. The recorded text still changes, so this exercises the same behavior.\n- \"Without running a model\" is not asserted directly. `validate_repository` is a purely local check, and the test shows the changed input is reported without any reassessment.\n\nI found no gaps worth failing the example.",
